In supergravity-extended models of the Minimal Supersymmetric Standard Model (MSSM), the graviton has a fermionic superpartner called the gravitino, whose mass m_{3/2} and couplings to MSSM fields are determined by the scale of spontaneous supersymmetry breaking (with the longitudinal/goldstino components coupling more strongly at low gravitino mass). In the early universe, gravitinos can be produced thermally through 2→2 scattering processes involving gauge and gaugino fields in a hot plasma at temperature T (with a production rate roughly proportional to the reheating temperature T_R after inflation), as well as non-thermally through the decays of heavier MSSM superpartners.

Consider the following cosmological constraints:
(i) the present energy density of stable relics must satisfy Ω h² ≤ Ω_obs h² (no overclosure of the universe);
(ii) relativistic gravitinos must not contribute excess effective relativistic degrees of freedom inconsistent with CMB and BBN observations;
(iii) decays of unstable gravitinos (or of the next-to-lightest superpartner into gravitinos) at times t ≳ 1 s must not dissociate light nuclei nor distort the CMB beyond observational limits;
(iv) supernova SN1987A energy-loss constraints on light gravitino emission must be respected;
(v) any baryon asymmetry must be generated by a mechanism (e.g. leptogenesis, electroweak baryogenesis, Affleck–Dine) compatible with the allowed reheating temperature and with any late dilution.

Problem. Determine, as a function of the gravitino mass m_{3/2} (ranging from eV scales in gauge-mediated scenarios up to ~TeV scales in gravity-mediated scenarios) and the reheating temperature T_R after primordial inflation:

(a) the regions of the (m_{3/2}, T_R) plane that simultaneously satisfy constraints (i)–(iv);
(b) for those allowed regions, identify which baryogenesis mechanisms remain viable, accounting for possible dilution by a late short period of secondary inflation that would also dilute any pre-existing baryon asymmetry;
(c) decide whether, within all phenomenologically acceptable supersymmetry-breaking scenarios, a consistent cosmological history exists, and characterize it.

